timestamp=2026-11-04T19:15:40.221Z level=INFO run=2db649e5 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# If PAT_033 received bereavement compensation, maybe PAT_033 is the claimant (family member) after someone died?\n# Or is the deceased person the one for whom compensation is paid? \n# That depends on semantics. But typically bereavement/family support is paid to survivors. \n# Also looking at patterns: who are the people with no claims but heavily listed as EC?\n# And also people who have inactive coverage + certain claim types?\n\n# Also look at those with no claims and are ECs: top ones are PAT_203 (3), PAT_267 (3), PAT_273 (3), etc. - could be deceased relatives/contacts\n# Compare: PAT_203 has no claims, 3 people list him as EC. Is he deceased? \n# Similarly check if any of these \"no claim but EC\" patients have something odd about them.\n# Also look at all records where a patient appears as EC and the other has claims - maybe the \"EC\" is a next of kin. If the EC themselves has no claims and appears often, possibly deceased.\n\n# Another hypothesis: deceased patients might have no claim records (or have records that indicate death). Also bereavement was paid to PAT_033 - so maybe someone related to PAT_033 died? Or PAT_033 died? Unclear. Let us look at the full picture.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
