timestamp=2026-11-04T19:15:58.856Z level=INFO run=2db649e5 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# Build network: for each patient, are they claimants? Who do they list as EC?\n# Also count how many dependents/claimants list each person as EC\nec_to_count = {}\nec_to_listers = []\nfor r in rows:\n    ec = r['emergency_contact_patient_id']\n    if ec:\n        ec_to_count[ec] = ec_to_count.get(ec, 0) + 1\n\n# Patients with no claims\nclaims = set(r['patient_id'] for r in rows if r['record_date'])\nall_pats = set(r['patient_id'] for r in rows)\nno_claims = all_pats - claims\n\n# Deceased people often: have no records of receiving care themselves, but others list them as emergency contact (next of kin is contacted about them? Or they're the contact). \n# Or sometimes if someone dies, their policy might have payouts. Also bereavement is paid to someone else.\n# Another interpretation: look at who the bereavement/family support was for? Maybe the reason implies the subject - but the data just has compensation_reason on the claim record.\n# Also \"direct_payment_usd\" goes to whom? The record's patient_id is the claimant/recipient? Possibly the patient_id is the person associated with the claim. \n# So PAT_033 has a bereavement claim - maybe PAT_033 is the deceased, and payment goes to beneficiaries? Or maybe PAT_033 is the beneficiary. \n# But also PAT_033 has no other claims except bereavement. And others list PAT_033 as EC - that’s odd if PAT_033 is deceased. Or if PAT_033 is a surviving relative receiving bereavement, that’s possible.\n# Also look up names for context if any, but probably not. Let us look at patterns more broadly.\n\n# Also look at inactive coverage patients - PAT_169, PAT_217, PAT_265 are inactive and have claims. Maybe inactive due to death? \n# Also PAT_037, PAT_252, PAT_276, PAT_134 have inactive coverage - some have claims, some? Check them.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
