timestamp=2026-11-04T19:16:30.499Z level=INFO run=2db649e5 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# Another angle: look for people who are ECs for patients that got large payouts related to death, or who themselves only appear as ECs and have no claims\n# Also consider patients who got bereavement/family support - maybe the \"deceased\" is someone else. Or look at who is not present as a patient with claims but is an important EC.\n# For example, PAT_203 appears as EC for 3 people but has no claims. That suggests PAT_203 might be the deceased person (contact person is now gone)? Or they might be unreachable/incapacitated.\n# Also compare: sometimes when a person dies, claims are filed. Or they become listed as deceased. \n# Another common pattern: the deceased has no claim records themselves, but there's a bereavement claim. Also maybe the bereavement claim is paid to the spouse - so the claimant (patient_id on bereavement record) might be the living spouse. That spouse might list the deceased as EC? Or vice versa.\n\n# Look at who lists PAT_011 (EC for PAT_033 who got bereavement) as EC? Or look up PAT_011\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
