timestamp=2026-11-04T19:16:57.431Z level=INFO run=2db649e5 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# So PAT_033 (bereavement, got 102700) lists PAT_011 as EC. And PAT_033 is also listed as EC by 4 others. That’s confusing.\n# Another interpretation: maybe the deceased are the ones who appear as emergency contacts but have no claim records themselves, AND perhaps there are bereavement/family claims associated with them?\n# For example, maybe someone died and family filed claims. The deceased person might be the \"subject\" but not a claimant. Is the deceased person in the patient list at all as a patient_id? \n# Look at PAT_203 - no claims, appears as EC 3 times. Are there any claims related to PAT_203? Like family support/bereavement where the context involves PAT_203? Not directly. No mention.\n# Another possibility: people with no claims and who appear as EC for multiple patients, and also perhaps those EC'd patients have large payouts? Or just the \"orphaned\" EC role - could indicate death.\n# Also look at documentation_status, coverage_status patterns. Or look at all \"no claims\" patients sorted by how many times they’re referenced as EC.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
