timestamp=2026-11-04T19:17:21.083Z level=INFO run=2db649e5 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# PAT_065 is listed as EC by PAT_265. PAT_265 received family support adjustment (101400). \n# That strongly suggests PAT_065 might be the deceased person (for whom family support is being provided).\n# Similarly, is there anyone else connected to a death-related payout where the \"subject\" is an EC?\n\n# Also look at PAT_033's case - bereavement paid to PAT_033. Who does PAT_033 list as EC? PAT_011. And who lists PAT_033 as EC? Multiple people. \n# Is it possible PAT_033 is the deceased? Then why does PAT_033 have the bereavement claim record under their own patient_id? That depends on data model. But \"bereavement compensation\" paid out - if the record is a claim/compensation record, the patient_id might be the recipient or the primary claimant. Or the deceased. That’s ambiguous.\n\n# Another interpretation: look for \"no claims themselves but others rely on them as EC\" + there’s a large family support/bereavement claim in the network involving them.\n# PAT_065 is EC of PAT_265; PAT_265 gets family support. That suggests the family support is because of something happening to someone connected to PAT_065? Or maybe PAT_065 is the policy holder/deceased. Or possibly the deceased is PAT_065.\n# Similarly, look for other such chains. Also check if PAT_065 themselves have any record at all - they appear only as EC, no claims.\n\n# Also look at other ECs. For example, PAT_084 has no claims, listed by 3 people - no death-related claims from listers. Same for others.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
