timestamp=2026-11-04T19:17:28.457Z level=INFO run=2db649e5 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# Look at all records more. Another pattern: sometimes deceased patients are marked differently. Or look at \"documentation_status\", \"coverage_status\" combinations. Also look for \"bereavement\" and \"family support\" - who are they paid to?\n# PAT_033 gets bereavement - and 4 others list PAT_033 as EC. If PAT_033 died, why would others list PAT_033 as their emergency contact? That doesn't make sense. That means PAT_033 is not the deceased; PAT_033 is a contact. So who died? Maybe the person related to the bereavement claim - but not named explicitly as patient_id? Or the deceased is someone else in the family. That’s odd.\n# Or perhaps the bereavement is for a dependent of PAT_033? Unclear from this schema.\n\n# Another look at PAT_065: PAT_265 lists PAT_065 as EC and PAT_265 gets family support. Maybe PAT_065 is the deceased. Also PAT_065 has no claims, no other records. That fits - deceased people don't file claims themselves.\n# Another case: look at PAT_273 - PAT_273 is EC for 3 people (PAT_038, PAT_061, PAT_081). PAT_038 has workplace fall injury, others have claims. No bereavement/family support. Also PAT_273 has no claims. Is PAT_273 deceased? Could be. Similarly others with high EC counts and no claims.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
