timestamp=2026-11-04T19:18:10.952Z level=INFO run=2db649e5 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# PAT_169 themselves have inactive coverage and have a claim. Also 4 people list PAT_169 as EC. That could mean PAT_169 is elderly/incapacitated or possibly deceased? Also \"outpatient soreness review\" claim and inactive coverage - maybe related.\n# Similarly PAT_217 has inactive coverage, has claim, and is listed as EC by 4 others (PAT_273, PAT_247, PAT_028, PAT_190). Also PAT_217 lists PAT_146 as EC. So PAT_217 is someone with inactive coverage but has claims - could be deceased? Or just inactive policy.\n# This is ambiguous. But the question says \"most likely to be deceased\" and gives us a specific dataset. Let us look for all clues.\n\n# Another clue: bereavement compensation is paid out - usually paid when someone dies. Who is the subject? If the patient_id on bereavement record is the recipient (survivor), then the deceased is someone else. Also look at the full row again: \"bereavement compensation\" - direct payment of 102700 to PAT_033. And others list PAT_033 as EC. That suggests PAT_033 is alive (receiving payment, listed as EC). So the deceased is probably a relative of PAT_033. But the deceased is not listed as a patient in the dataset? That could be. Or perhaps the deceased is another patient_id we need to identify.\n# Similarly for family support adjustment paid to PAT_265 - PAT_265 lists PAT_065 as EC. Maybe PAT_065 is the deceased. That fits perfectly. Also PAT_065 has no claims, no own record except being an EC target? Let us see - PAT_065 only appears as emergency_contact_patient_id in other rows; does PAT_065 appear as a patient row with their own info? Look back at the full dataset structure.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
