timestamp=2026-11-04T19:18:53.549Z level=INFO run=2db649e5 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# Build full picture\n# For PAT_033 (bereavement recipient): lists EC as PAT_011. Others who list PAT_033 as EC are: PAT_129, PAT_221, PAT_174, PAT_069. \n# PAT_174 has no claim record (empty fields). PAT_129, PAT_221, PAT_069 all have claims (non-bereavement). That means those 4 people consider PAT_033 their EC.\n# Also PAT_033 is the one receiving bereavement payment. That’s consistent with PAT_033 being a surviving family member. Who died? Maybe PAT_174 (who has no claims and lists PAT_033 as EC)? Or maybe someone else. Or maybe the deceased is not a patient in this registry? That’s possible. But let us look up PAT_174.\nfor r in rows:\n    if r['patient_id'] == 'PAT_174':\n        print(r)\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
