timestamp=2026-11-04T19:19:37.951Z level=INFO run=2db649e5 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# Only two death-related payouts: bereavement (PAT_033) and family support (PAT_265).\n# Connected deceased candidates from those: PAT_174 (connected to PAT_033 via EC relationship, no claims) and PAT_065 (connected to PAT_265, no claims).\n# That gives us 2 likely deceased. We need 3 total.\n# So we need one more. Who else is most likely deceased?\n\n# Look for other patterns: patients with no claims who are heavily listed as EC and maybe have inactive coverage? Or large clusters.\n# For example, PAT_203 appears as EC 3 times, no claims, exists as patient. Anyone connected to death payouts? No. But maybe deceased. Also PAT_267 appears as EC 3 times, no claims. Any connection? No bereavement/family support involving them as the subject. But could be deceased.\n# Also look at inactive coverage patients - PAT_169 is inactive with claims (and 4 people list them as EC). That’s possible if they’re incapacitated or deceased. Similarly PAT_217 inactive with claims, 4 people list them as EC. Also PAT_252, PAT_276, PAT_134, PAT_037 inactive - they all have claims. That’s less like \"deceased\" (they’re receiving care). \n# Compare: deceased people typically don’t have treatment claims under their own ID after death? But data is a snapshot. Still, having no claims at all is a stronger signal than having claims but inactive coverage.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
