timestamp=2026-11-04T19:21:01.859Z level=INFO run=2db649e5 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# So PAT_174 lists bereavement recipient PAT_033 as EC and has no claims → deceased. Confirmed.\n# PAT_023 lists family support recipient PAT_265 as EC and has no claims → also likely deceased.\n# That’s a third candidate!\n# Let us verify the connections for PAT_023.\n\n# Also check the other direction we found: PAT_065 is EC of PAT_265 (family support recipient). That means PAT_065 is the subject of family support? Or related differently. In that case, if the deceased is PAT_065, then family member PAT_265 receives support. So PAT_065 (deceased) is an EC of PAT_265? That doesn’t fit - usually the living relative is the EC. Or maybe the EC field represents different semantics? Or maybe PAT_065 is the policy holder who died, and PAT_265 is the claimant/beneficiary who lists PAT_065 as their EC? Or could be reversed in meaning.\n\n# But also PAT_023 lists PAT_265 as EC and has no claims. If PAT_023 is deceased, then maybe PAT_265 is their EC/contact? That would mean PAT_265 is alive and is the contact of the deceased PAT_023. Also PAT_265 receives family support. That suggests PAT_265 is the beneficiary/recipient of family support due to PAT_023’s death? That’s plausible if PAT_265 is the next of kin/guardian receiving support.\n# That gives us PAT_023 as another deceased person. That’s a strong candidate for the third.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
