timestamp=2026-11-04T19:22:45.391Z level=INFO run=2db649e5 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# PAT_178 and PAT_146 both have no claims and list PAT_065 as their EC. Also PAT_065 (the EC) has no claims. \n# That means a cluster of people with no claims all pointing to PAT_065 as EC. Also PAT_265 (who does have a claim and receives family support) also lists PAT_065 as EC. \n# That’s a very strong signal that PAT_065 is the central person (likely deceased) - others list them as contact. Also family support is paid to PAT_265. That fits the hypothesis that PAT_065 is deceased.\n# Compare to others - for example, PAT_174 has only 1 lister (PAT_032) and PAT_032 has no claims either. That suggests PAT_174 might also be deceased (and PAT_032, the one listing them, might also be affected/deceased? Or just a dependent with no claims). Also PAT_174 is linked to bereavement paid to PAT_033. That still points to PAT_174 being deceased.\n# And PAT_023 similarly - only lister is PAT_040 with no claims, and PAT_023 is linked to family support recipient PAT_265.\n\n# So we have multiple candidates. But we need exactly 3. Which 3 are \"most likely\"?\n# The problem gives us a scoring function at the end - Jaccard index against ground truth. But we don’t see ground truth. Maybe we need to infer from the most obvious signals.\n# The strongest signals are: (1) death-related compensation paid + direct connection. We have two such death-related payouts: bereavement to PAT_033 (connected to PAT_174 as deceased) and family support to PAT_265 (connected to PAT_065 as deceased). Also PAT_023 connects to family support recipient PAT_265 as someone who lists PAT_265 as EC and has no claims. That’s another plausible deceased.\n# So candidates from explicit death-related financial records give us: PAT_174 (via bereavement), and PAT_065 and/or PAT_023 (via family support). That’s 3 people total: PAT_174, PAT_065, PAT_023.\n# That fits perfectly as the top 3. Let us check this combination against other possibilities.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
