timestamp=2026-11-04T19:23:32.489Z level=INFO run=2db649e5 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv') as f:\n    reader = csv.DictReader(f)\n    rows = list(reader)\n\n# Alternative: the deceased might include people who have no claims and are ECs for many people (high centrality), regardless of payout links?\n# For example, PAT_267 has 3 listers, all with claims. No payout links. Is it possible they’re deceased? Maybe, but less certain than those with explicit bereavement/family support connections.\n# Another angle - look at the actual payouts and see if there’s any other clue. Both death-related payouts are very large (102700, 101400). That suggests significant events.\n# Also PAT_023, PAT_174, PAT_065 all have no claims, no record_date, no compensation info - consistent with being deceased.\n# This seems plausible. Let us also look at one more thing - who are the other listers of recipients?\n# For family support paid to PAT_265 - PAT_265’s own record shows they have family support adjustment. But who else might be covered? And the family support might be due to deaths of multiple family members? That could explain PAT_023 also being connected. Or maybe PAT_065 is the primary deceased and PAT_023 is another? Or vice versa.\n# Also PAT_023 lists PAT_265 as EC and is listed as EC by PAT_040 (who has no claims). That forms a chain. Similarly PAT_174 is in a chain with PAT_032 (no claims). PAT_065 is in a cluster with multiple no-claim listers. These all look like deceased clusters.\n# This combination {PAT_023, PAT_065, PAT_174} is consistent with the explicit death-related compensation records in the data.\n# Let us write the answer as these three.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
